export function portability(add,cpp){
add('cpp-standards','standard-baseline','State the language baseline explicitly',
'A language version controls available syntax and semantic rules. __cplusplus reports the selected language mode, not the completeness of every standard-library feature. This course requires C++20 or later.\n\nA project should document its compiler, language mode, library, and host separately so a successful build has an interpretable contract.',
cpp('iostream','    static_assert(__cplusplus>=202002L);\n    std::cout<<"C++20 baseline enabled\\n";'),'C++20 baseline enabled',
'The compile-time assertion rejects an older selected language mode before the program runs.',
'A recent compiler executable can still be invoked in an older language mode.',
['What does __cplusplus primarily describe?','The selected C++ language mode','The operating-system version','Every library implementation detail'],'The mode is distinct from compiler and library release numbers.',
'Compile the exported source with C++17 and C++20 flags and compare the baseline diagnostic. Record the toolchain versions separately.');
add('cpp-standards','feature-test','Detect a required facility with its feature macro',
'Feature-test macros describe availability of particular language or library facilities. Include the relevant header or version header, then test the documented macro value rather than guessing from a vendor name.\n\nFeature detection should guard an intentional fallback or an explicit build rejection. Silently removing behavior can change a lesson or application contract.',
cpp('iostream span version','    static_assert(__cpp_lib_span>=202002L);\n    int values[]{1,2};std::span<int> view(values);\n    std::cout<<"span="<<view.size()<<"\\n";'),'span=2',
'The program checks span availability and then uses the facility covered by that requirement.',
'A compiler version check can mispredict a feature supplied by a separately versioned standard library.',
['Why prefer a facility feature macro?','It directly describes the required facility','It proves every facility is complete','It detects all runtime platform behavior'],'The check targets the capability the code needs.',
'Add a documented required macro for concepts or ranges. Distinguish a missing facility from a runtime host limitation.');
add('cpp-standards','required-headers','Include the header that declares each used facility',
'A standard header can include other headers as an implementation detail. Depending on that accidental inclusion can make code compile with one library and fail with another. Include the declarations you actually use.\n\nSmall translation-unit checks are useful: compile a public header on its own with a minimal caller rather than only through a large precompiled environment.',
cpp('cstddef iostream vector',"    std::vector<int> values{1, 2, 3};\n    std::size_t size = values.size();\n    std::cout << \"elements=\" << size << \"\\n\";"),'elements=3',
'vector declares the container, cstddef supplies size_t, and iostream supplies the output interface.',
'An umbrella header in one environment can hide missing direct includes in your own header.',
['Should a public header rely on an unrelated caller’s includes?','No','Yes','Only for templates'],'Its declared dependencies should be self-contained.',
'Create a tiny source that includes only one project header and main. Compile it with two standard-library/toolchain combinations when available.');
add('cpp-standards','sequencing','Make effect ordering explicit',
'Expressions with side effects require a sequencing rule. Rather than relying on subtle evaluation-order details, split logically ordered operations into statements when the algorithm needs one result before another.\n\nThis is especially useful around stream reads, state mutation, and function calls. Readability can make a correctness argument easier than a compact expression.',
cpp('iostream',"    int value = 1;\n    int before = value;\n    ++value;\n    int after = value;\n    std::cout << before << \" \" << after << \"\\n\";"),'1 2',
'Each full expression completes before the next statement, so the before and after values have an explicit order.',
'Do not infer a universal argument-evaluation order from one compiler’s observed output.',
['What establishes the order in this example?','Separate sequenced statements','Alphabetical variable names','The output buffer size'],'Each completed statement precedes the next.',
'Audit an input-processing expression with several side effects and rewrite it as named steps with a stated precondition for each.');
add('cpp-standards','object-abi','Object representation is not an interchange format',
'C++ object layout can include padding and implementation-specific representations. Standard-layout properties help certain language operations, but do not turn an object containing a string into a portable file record.\n\nA stable interchange format defines widths, byte order, text encoding, and validation independently of ABI choices.',
cpp('array cstdint iostream',"    std::array<unsigned char, 4> bytes{0x78, 0x56, 0x34, 0x12};\n    std::uint32_t value = 0;\n    for (unsigned i = 0; i < 4; ++i)\n        value |= std::uint32_t(bytes[i]) << (8 * i);\n    std::cout << std::hex << value << \"\\n\";"),'12345678',
'The value is reconstructed from explicit octets without reinterpreting the array as a uint32_t object.',
'reinterpret_cast does not supply alignment, lifetime, or endianness guarantees for arbitrary file bytes.',
['What makes these bytes portable?','The explicitly defined decoding rule','The address of the array','The current struct padding'],'The format contract is independent of host object layout.',
'Write the matching encoder and independent fixtures. Keep ABI-dependent structs out of the persisted representation.');

add('numeric-boundaries','mixed-sign','Compare signed and unsigned values deliberately',
'Usual arithmetic conversions can turn a negative signed value into an unsigned value during comparison. C++20 comparison helpers express mathematical ordering across signedness without relying on that surprising conversion.\n\nThe right type still depends on the domain. Do not use a negative sentinel in a size field when optional or a named status can represent absence.',
cpp('iostream utility',"    int negative = -1;\n    unsigned positive = 1;\n    std::cout << \"less=\" << std::cmp_less(negative, positive)\n              << \" equal=\" << std::cmp_equal(negative, positive) << \"\\n\";"),'less=1 equal=0',
'The helper correctly reports that minus one is less than positive one.',
'Casting the negative value to unsigned before comparison changes its mathematical meaning.',
['What does cmp_less preserve across signedness?','Mathematical value ordering','The wrapped unsigned representation','A pointer ordering'],'The helper handles the sign difference explicitly.',
'Test zero, negative one, and unsigned maximum. Compare results with a deliberately stated domain policy for missing values.');
add('numeric-boundaries','checked-narrowing','Validate representability before narrowing',
'A narrower destination type may not represent the source value. std::in_range checks representability for supported integer types before an explicit cast commits the conversion.\n\nClamping, rejecting, and wrapping are different policies. The example chooses rejection so callers cannot confuse a truncated value with a valid field.',
cpp('cstdint iostream optional utility',"    for (unsigned value : {255u, 256u}) {\n        auto result = narrow(value);\n        std::cout << value << \":\" << bool(result) << \"\\n\";\n    }","std::optional<std::uint8_t> narrow(unsigned value) {\n    if (!std::in_range<std::uint8_t>(value))\n        return std::nullopt;\n    return static_cast<std::uint8_t>(value);\n}"),'255:1\n256:0',
'255 fits the eight-bit field and 256 does not. The rejected value is never cast into an accepted result.',
'Checking whether a value changed after a lossy cast can already have discarded the information needed by the contract.',
['When is the cast performed?','After the range check succeeds','Before checking the source','Even when the function returns no result'],'Representability is established before conversion.',
'Add a signed source version and test negative values. Keep the application’s field bounds separate from the destination type’s range.');
add('numeric-boundaries','checked-multiply','Check multiplication without overflowing the check',
'For nonnegative unsigned values, multiplication fits when one factor is zero or the other does not exceed max divided by the first. This rearranges the precondition without computing the potentially overflowing product.\n\nUnsigned wrapping is defined, but that does not make a wrapped row count or allocation size acceptable to the application.',
cpp('iostream limits optional',"    std::cout << *multiply(6, 7)\n              << \" overflow=\" << !multiply(std::numeric_limits<unsigned>::max(), 2)\n              << \"\\n\";","std::optional<unsigned> multiply(unsigned a, unsigned b) {\n    if (a != 0 && b > std::numeric_limits<unsigned>::max() / a)\n        return std::nullopt;\n    return a * b;\n}"),'42 overflow=1',
'The small product is accepted; the maximum value times two is rejected before multiplication.',
'Checking a*b against max after unsigned wrapping does not recover the original mathematical product.',
['Why handle a==0 separately?','To avoid division by zero in the guard','Because zero overflows multiplication','To force a signed conversion'],'The guard itself must be valid for every permitted input.',
'Test zero, one, maximum, and factors immediately around a boundary. Apply the same discipline to byte-count calculations.');
add('numeric-boundaries','checked-division','Division has more than a zero-denominator boundary',
'Signed integer division must reject zero denominators and the minimum-value divided by minus one case when that quotient is not representable. Negating the minimum value has the same representability problem.\n\nValidate before performing the operation. A CPU trap or a Wasm trap is not an ordinary C++ error result that the caller can safely ignore.',
cpp('iostream limits optional',"    std::cout << *divide(9, 2) << \" zero=\" << !divide(1, 0)\n              << \" minimum=\" << !divide(std::numeric_limits<int>::min(), -1) << \"\\n\";","std::optional<int> divide(int a, int b) {\n    if (b == 0 || (a == std::numeric_limits<int>::min() && b == -1))\n        return std::nullopt;\n    return a / b;\n}"),'4 zero=1 minimum=1',
'Integer division truncates nine divided by two to four. Both exceptional inputs are rejected before division.',
'A nonzero denominator alone is not a sufficient precondition for all signed division inputs.',
['Which nonzero denominator needs a special minimum-value check?','Minus one','One','Two'],'The positive quotient would exceed the signed maximum.',
'Add checked remainder and checked negation with the corresponding exceptional-value rules. Test them independently of division.');
add('numeric-boundaries','floating-domain','Validate finite floating values and choose a tolerance policy',
'Floating-point arithmetic can produce rounding differences, infinities, and NaNs. A domain that accepts only finite measurements must enforce that rule separately from syntactic numeric parsing.\n\nApproximate comparison needs an application-specific tolerance policy. This example uses a small absolute tolerance for a fixed-scale demonstration, not a universal equality replacement.',
cpp('cmath iostream limits','    double value=0.1+0.2;\n    bool close=std::abs(value-0.3)<1e-12;\n    auto nan=std::numeric_limits<double>::quiet_NaN();\n    std::cout<<"close="<<close<<" finite_nan="<<std::isfinite(nan)<<" self_equal="<<(nan==nan)<<"\\n";'),'close=1 finite_nan=0 self_equal=0',
'The finite sum is close under the chosen tolerance. NaN is nonfinite and is not equal to itself.',
'An absolute tolerance alone can be unsuitable across very different numeric scales.',
['Does NaN compare equal to itself?','No','Yes','Only when printed first'],'NaN has special comparison behavior.',
'Define finite-input, relative-tolerance, and near-zero policies for a measurement domain before writing its parser and tests.');

add('host-files-text','line-endings','Normalize line endings at a text boundary',
'Text files may use LF or CRLF line endings. getline removes LF from the stream but can leave a preceding carriage return in the returned string when reading literal bytes. A text protocol must decide how to normalize that boundary.\n\nDo not apply text normalization to a binary database format, where every byte has a defined role.',
cpp('iostream sstream string',"    std::istringstream input(\"LIST\\r\\nSAVE\\n\");\n    std::string line;\n    while (std::getline(input, line)) {\n        if (!line.empty() && line.back() == '\\r')\n            line.pop_back();\n        std::cout << \"[\" << line << \"]\\n\";\n    }"),'[LIST]\n[SAVE]',
'The CRLF and LF lines produce the same command text after the explicit trailing-CR rule.',
'Blindly deleting every carriage return can alter quoted payload text rather than just line framing.',
['Where should this normalization happen?','At the documented text framing boundary','Inside every binary field','After converting the entire file to native structs'],'The protocol defines which delimiters are framing rather than payload.',
'Test empty lines and a final line without a terminator. Document whether embedded carriage returns are valid payload or rejected controls.');
add('host-files-text','binary-zero','Binary payloads can contain zero bytes',
'A binary stream carries explicit byte counts, including zero-valued bytes. C string functions that stop at a zero terminator cannot represent arbitrary binary payload lengths.\n\nUse a container size and a checked write/read count. The database format’s string fields have their own explicit lengths and validation rules.',
cpp('iostream sstream string',"    std::string bytes(\"A\\0B\", 3);\n    std::istringstream input(bytes);\n    char value;\n    unsigned count = 0, zeros = 0;\n    while (input.get(value)) {\n        ++count;\n        if (value == 0)\n            ++zeros;\n    }\n    std::cout << \"bytes=\" << count << \" zeros=\" << zeros << \"\\n\";"),'bytes=3 zeros=1',
'All three bytes are consumed, including the embedded zero between A and B.',
'strlen would report only the prefix before the embedded zero.',
['What supplies the binary length here?','The explicit string size','A zero terminator','The printed width'],'The owned byte sequence tracks its complete length.',
'Write and reopen the same three bytes in binary mode. Compare exact bytes and distinguish end-of-file from a zero byte.');
add('host-files-text','text-byte-slices','A Unicode boundary is not an arbitrary byte position',
'UTF-8 uses continuation bytes for multibyte code points. A byte-oriented storage format can count bytes while still requiring valid text at field boundaries. Never assume a maximum byte count can be enforced by cutting at that position blindly.\n\nThis fixture exposes the two bytes of one code point; full validation also checks overlong encodings, surrogates, and maximum scalar values.',
cpp('iomanip iostream string',"    std::string text = \"ë\";\n    std::cout << \"bytes=\" << text.size() << \" lead=\" << std::hex\n              << unsigned(static_cast<unsigned char>(text[0]))\n              << \" continuation=\" << unsigned(static_cast<unsigned char>(text[1]))\n              << \"\\n\";"),'bytes=2 lead=c3 continuation=ab',
'The two-byte sequence must remain together when forming a valid text field.',
'Checking only that continuation bytes have the right prefix is not complete UTF-8 validation.',
['Can either individual byte represent this character alone?','No','Yes','Only after changing the locale'],'The code point is encoded by the complete multibyte sequence.',
'Inspect a three-byte and a four-byte code point. Compare valid boundaries with the capstone’s byte-length limit.');
add('host-files-text','path-contracts','Keep logical names separate from host path rules',
'Paths are host interfaces with rules about separators, case, encoding, and replacement behavior. A portable storage design can restrict logical names and implement host adaptation separately. Lexical normalization is not a security proof that a path stays inside a filesystem root.\n\nThis native lesson normalizes a path without asserting identical filesystem case behavior across hosts.',
cpp('filesystem iostream',"    std::filesystem::path path(\"logs/../people.cpf\");\n    std::cout << path.lexically_normal().generic_string() << \"\\n\";"),'people.cpf',
'The lexical parent component cancels logs. No filesystem lookup or symlink resolution is performed.',
'A symlink can make lexical containment differ from actual filesystem resolution.',
['Does lexically_normal resolve symlinks?','No','Yes','Only for relative paths'],'It transforms path syntax without querying the filesystem.',
'Define allowed database basenames and reject traversal components before passing names to a host adapter.',{native:true,reason:'This investigation uses the native filesystem path interface.'});
add('host-files-text','eof-state','EOF is a stream state, not a stored byte',
'A stream read can fail because it reached the end or because an I/O error occurred. A parser should distinguish successful extraction of the final value from the subsequent failed read that establishes EOF.\n\nChecking eof before reading does not predict whether the next read succeeds. Drive processing from the extraction operation itself.',
cpp('iostream sstream',"    std::istringstream input(\"AB\");\n    char value;\n    unsigned count = 0;\n    while (input.get(value))\n        ++count;\n    std::cout << \"count=\" << count << \" eof=\" << input.eof() << \" bad=\" << input.bad()\n              << \"\\n\";"),'count=2 eof=1 bad=0',
'Two successful reads process A and B. The next attempt reaches end-of-input without a bad I/O state.',
'while(!stream.eof()) can process stale data after a failed extraction.',
['What controls this loop?','Whether extraction succeeds','A pre-read eof prediction','The last character being zero'],'Only a successful read contributes a processed byte.',
'Test empty input and inject an error state in a separate stream fixture. Specify which failures may retain a previous candidate.');

add('storage-audit','audit-octets','Make byte-width assumptions executable',
'A byte format defined in octets requires eight-bit char units and the stated fixed-width integer types. Reject unsupported implementations at compile time rather than silently writing a different format.\n\nThese checks establish scalar representation assumptions only. Filesystem atomicity, durability, encoding, and concurrency remain separate contracts.',
cpp('climits cstdint iostream limits','    static_assert(CHAR_BIT==8);static_assert(std::numeric_limits<std::uint32_t>::digits==32);\n    std::cout<<"octets=8 uint32=32\\n";'),'octets=8 uint32=32',
'The selected implementation satisfies the explicit CPF1 scalar requirements.',
'A fixed-width integer declaration does not specify file byte order automatically.',
['What does this assertion establish?','The selected implementation’s scalar widths','Power-loss durability','All input-file validity'],'It checks a compile-time implementation precondition.',
'List every additional assumption the capstone makes beyond these scalar widths and assign each an appropriate check.');
add('storage-audit','audit-endianness','Compare against independently specified file bytes',
'A portability audit should include an external byte fixture that every producer emits and every consumer accepts. A cross-host exchange matrix adds evidence that independent environments agree on the same contract.\n\nThis executable is one local exact-byte check. It is not presented as a completed matrix across operating systems that have not been tested.',
cpp('array cstdint iostream',"    std::uint32_t value = 256;\n    std::array<unsigned char, 4> actual{};\n    for (unsigned i = 0; i < 4; ++i)\n        actual[i] = static_cast<unsigned char>((value >> (8 * i)) & 255u);\n    const std::array<unsigned char, 4> expected{0, 1, 0, 0};\n    std::cout << \"fixture=\" << (actual == expected) << \"\\n\";\n    return actual == expected ? 0 : 1;"),'fixture=1',
'The literal fixture identifies little-endian 256 without consulting the decoder.',
'Calling a local roundtrip a cross-host compatibility test overstates the evidence.',
['What is still needed for a real exchange matrix?','Files from each producer tested by each consumer','Only a second call to the same encoder','A larger screenshot'],'Independent hosts must actually exchange the format fixtures.',
'Export a CPF1 file on two hosts and run both consumers against both files. Retain the exact bytes and toolchain versions.');
add('storage-audit','audit-header-rejection','Reject unsupported headers before allocating row storage',
'Header and count checks should occur before allocating resources based on untrusted fields. An unsupported magic or format version has no valid interpretation under the current decoder.\n\nFailure preservation includes leaving the existing live table unchanged while reporting a rejected import.',
cpp('array iostream',"    const std::array<unsigned char, 4> header{'C', 'P', 'F', '2'};\n    bool accepted = header == std::array<unsigned char, 4>{'C', 'P', 'F', '1'};\n    unsigned live_rows = 3;\n    if (accepted)\n        live_rows = 0;\n    std::cout << \"accepted=\" << accepted << \" retained=\" << live_rows << \"\\n\";"),'accepted=0 retained=3',
'The future-version header is rejected before the live row count is touched.',
'Treating all versions as equivalent can misparse later fields and overwrite valid state.',
['When should unsupported format rejection occur?','Before interpreting dependent fields','After replacing the live table','Only when printing rows fails'],'Early format recognition establishes the decoding rules.',
'Test short headers and unrelated magic values. Verify no table-allocation or mutation callback runs for rejected headers.');
add('storage-audit','audit-length-arithmetic','Audit bounds checks for overflow in the checks themselves',
'A check such as offset+length <= size can overflow before it compares. Establish offset <= size first, then require length <= size-offset. The reordered guard is meaningful only with that first precondition.\n\nUse hostile boundary values in tests, including a maximum size_t length, instead of exercising only plausible small records.',
cpp('cstddef iostream limits',"    auto fits = [](std::size_t size, std::size_t offset, std::size_t length) {\n        return offset <= size && length <= size - offset;\n    };\n    std::cout << fits(8, 4, 4) << \" \" << fits(8, 9, 0) << \" \"\n              << fits(8, 1, std::numeric_limits<std::size_t>::max()) << \"\\n\";"),'1 0 0',
'The valid final four bytes fit. An out-of-range offset and a maximal length are rejected without wrapping an addition.',
'Subtracting before checking offset can replace an addition overflow bug with unsigned underflow.',
['Which condition must precede size-offset?','offset<=size','length==0','offset is odd'],'It proves the subtraction is representable in the intended range.',
'Enumerate small size/offset/length combinations and compare with an arbitrary-precision mathematical oracle.');
add('storage-audit','audit-duplicates','Uniqueness must be checked during import too',
'An insertion API can enforce unique keys while a separate importer accidentally bypasses that rule. Every ingestion boundary must establish the same logical invariants before publishing state.\n\nBuild a candidate key set or table during import and reject a duplicate rather than silently overwriting the first payload.',
cpp('iostream set vector',"    const std::vector<unsigned> imported{1, 2, 1};\n    std::set<unsigned> candidate;\n    bool accepted = true;\n    for (auto id : imported)\n        if (!candidate.insert(id).second) {\n            accepted = false;\n            break;\n        }\n    unsigned live_count = 4;\n    if (accepted)\n        live_count = static_cast<unsigned>(candidate.size());\n    std::cout << \"accepted=\" << accepted << \" retained=\" << live_count << \"\\n\";"),'accepted=0 retained=4',
'The third imported key is a duplicate, so the candidate is not published and the old live count remains four.',
'Using map[key]=row during import can conceal duplicate rows by replacing an earlier value.',
['Should duplicate import overwrite the earlier row here?','No, the format contract rejects it','Yes, always','Only when the score is larger'],'The importer must enforce the same uniqueness rule as insertion.',
'Test duplicates at the beginning and end, and pair them with truncated payloads. Verify the entire live table remains unchanged.');
add('storage-audit','audit-trailing-bytes','Complete consumption is part of format validation',
'A binary decoder must decide whether extra bytes after the documented record sequence are permitted. CPF1 rejects them, preventing an otherwise valid prefix from hiding unknown extensions or accidental concatenation.\n\nKeep the cursor and input size as separate evidence at the final publication boundary.',
cpp('cstddef iostream',"    auto complete = [](std::size_t consumed, std::size_t size) {\n        return consumed == size;\n    };\n    std::cout << \"exact=\" << complete(8, 8) << \" trailing=\" << complete(8, 9) << \"\\n\";"),'exact=1 trailing=0',
'Exactly consuming the empty-table header is accepted; one extra byte violates the current format contract.',
'A decoder that ignores trailing bytes silently accepts a broader format than the writer and documentation define.',
['When can the candidate be published?','After exact consumption and all other checks','After any valid prefix','Whenever the last byte is nonzero'],'Complete-file validation is a prerequisite for publication.',
'Append every one-byte suffix to a valid capstone fixture and verify each is rejected without changing the saved snapshot.');
add('storage-audit','audit-rename','Check host replacement behavior rather than assuming it',
'Snapshot publication often writes a candidate and renames it over an existing destination. Whether that replacement succeeds is a host-interface contract, not a universal consequence of using C++ streams.\n\nThis native POSIX fixture checks replacement and reopening on the selected host. A portable Windows adapter may need a different publication operation; neither path alone proves crash durability.',
cpp('cstdio fstream iostream string','    {std::ofstream old("current.txt");old<<"old";}\n    {std::ofstream next("candidate.txt");next<<"new";}\n    if(std::rename("candidate.txt","current.txt")!=0)return 1;\n    std::ifstream input("current.txt");std::string value;input>>value;\n    std::cout<<value<<"\\n";return value=="new"?0:1;'),'new',
'The host reports successful replacement, and reopening returns the candidate’s contents.',
'A checked rename is not equivalent to syncing file contents and the containing directory for a specified failure model.',
['What does this run establish?','Replacement/reopen behavior on this host','All-host power-loss durability','Atomic multi-file transactions'],'The evidence is scoped to the actual host operation exercised.',
'Add checked candidate close and injected rename failure. Record Windows and POSIX adapter behavior separately.',{native:true,reason:'File replacement semantics must be investigated on a native host.'});
add('storage-audit','audit-evidence','Keep implementation, tests, and guarantees separate',
'A useful audit lists each promised behavior and the evidence supporting it. “Implemented,” “tested on one host,” and “qualified across hosts” describe different levels of evidence. A finite happy-path suite cannot establish a power-loss guarantee.\n\nThis release gate fails if a required check is absent; it does not automatically promote untested assumptions into guarantees.',
cpp('array iostream string_view',"    const std::array<Check, 3> checks{{{\"exact bytes\", true, true},\n                                       {\"rejected import\", true, true},\n                                       {\"power loss\", false, false}}};\n    unsigned required_missing = 0;\n    for (const auto &check : checks) {\n        if (check.required && !check.passed)\n            ++required_missing;\n        std::cout << check.name << \":\" << (check.passed ? \"checked\" : \"not qualified\")\n                  << \"\\n\";\n    }\n    return required_missing ? 1 : 0;","struct Check {\n    std::string_view name;\n    bool required;\n    bool passed;\n};"),'exact bytes:checked\nrejected import:checked\npower loss:not qualified',
'The two required local checks pass; the unsupported power-loss claim remains explicitly unqualified.',
'Turning a limitation into a passing Boolean does not create supporting evidence.',
['What should happen to an unqualified durability claim?','It remains outside the stated guarantees','It is assumed true after compilation','It is hidden from the audit'],'A guarantee needs an explicit failure model and relevant evidence.',
'Write an audit table for CPF1 covering numeric fields, UTF-8, close failure, rename, browser storage, stale tabs, and crash behavior.');
}
